  "method": "Written from model knowledge only (no simulated nominations). Discovery-centred longlist for a merge with an independent GPT draft. Excludes achievements already rewarded in Chemistry 2016-2025 (molecular machines, cryo-EM, directed evolution and phage display, lithium-ion batteries, CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, asymmetric organocatalysis, click and bioorthogonal chemistry, quantum dots, computational protein design and structure prediction, metal-organic frameworks). 'nominees' is a provisional credit pool of at most three living people; 'other_names' holds further credited or historical contributors, including deceased pioneers, for the merge stage to weigh. Living status checked against Wikidata (and Wikipedia where Wikidata gave no usable match) on 2026-10-03; removed from nominee pools as deceased: Walter Kaminsky (2024), Hans-Herbert Brintzinger (2019), Axel D. Becke (2025), Josef Paldus (2023), John C. H. Spence (2021), Richard Neutze (2026), Richard A. Lerner (2021), Roger Sheldon (2025), Sigurd Hofmann (2022). A synthetic-genomics entry (Venter, Hamilton Smith, Hutchison) was dropped entirely after all three were found deceased (2026, 2025, 2025). Wikidata gave no usable entity for Hao Yan, Lei Wang, Thomas D. Madden, Andrew D. Griffiths, Mark E. Davis, Erick Carreira, Milton R. Smith III, Herman Overkleeft, Daniel G. Anderson, Anthony Turner and Bruce Dunn; Wikipedia showed no death for the ones checked and they are assumed living. No entry's credit pool contains a member of the 2026 Nobel Committee for Chemistry (checked against agent-data/chemistry/committee/ROSTER.md). REVIEW (claude-opus-5-5, 2026-10-03): corrected five other_names attributions (c06 Magliery -> Shigeyuki Yokoyama; c07 Yoshinori Ito -> Takumi Ito, the cereblon co-discoverer; c08 Hugh Rosen -> Eranthie Weerapana; c10 added Guifang Jia, Dan Dominissini and Kate D. Meyer in place of Christopher E. Mason; c21 replaced Shigeru Kuwata and Gregory C. Fu with Masanori Kosugi and Toshihiko Migita, who reported the first Pd-catalysed aminations; c52 Yasuhiko Ito -> John S. Wilkes and Michael J. Zaworotko) and added entries c70-c106 for omitted candidates. Further deaths found in the review and kept only in other_names: Masatake Haruta (2022), Christopher T. Walsh (2023), Josef Michl (2024), Fritz Eckstein (2026). Two drafted additions were dropped because their key figures have died: surface science of catalysis (Gabor A. Somorjai, 2025) and single-molecule electronics (Mark A. Ratner, 2026; Mark A. Reed, 2021). Not verifiable on Wikidata/Wikipedia, assumed living: Joseph A. Loo, Stephen W. Turner, Leo Gross, Gerhard Meyer, Holger Stark, Michael A. Robb, Massimo Olivucci, Tao Zhang, Jun Li (Tsinghua), Jesper Wengel, A. Stephen K. Hashmi, Gerhard Wagner (Harvard), George S. Wilson (Kansas). Committee overlaps: three candidate areas were deliberately left out because a 2026 committee member is in or adjacent to the credit pool: 3D electron diffraction / microED (Xiaodong Zou), free-energy simulation of biomolecules (Johan Aqvist), and proton pumping by cytochrome c oxidase (Peter Brzezinski). Two retained entries need a check at merge: c87 radical enzymology (Andrei Chabes works on ribonucleotide reductase regulation, though not in the pool) and c73 nanowires (Lars Samuelson, in other_names, is a Lund colleague and probable coauthor of chair Heiner Linke).",
